                <dc:creator>Fracchia Figueiredo, Myriam</dc:creator>
                <dc:date>2020-03-31</dc:date>
                <dc:description>&amp;lt;p&amp;gt;The growing process of violence in Mexico 2006 that is a result of the interpenetration of&amp;nbsp;what we call &amp;ldquo;criminal order&amp;rdquo; and &amp;ldquo;legal order&amp;rdquo; in the construction of the drug trafficking&amp;nbsp;monopoly, since at least 2006, tends to hide the historical process of selective&amp;nbsp;extermination of social activists, including environmental activists. This article presents&amp;nbsp;preliminary research results covering events of selective extermination recorded by&amp;nbsp;press between January 2017 and April 2019. The objective of the paper is to make&amp;nbsp;visible some characteristics of the process of violence that causes the gravest human&amp;nbsp;costs &amp;ndash; death, disappearance, or injuries, including torture and rape &amp;ndash; suffered by those&amp;nbsp;actors involved in struggles over water, land, forests or to prevent the construction of&amp;nbsp;megaprojects, including mining, which affect the collective rights of their communities.&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;</dc:description>
